okf_version: "1.0"
knowledge:
  task:
    id: "kevin-codex-stall-after-upgrade-20260830"
    date: "2026-08-30"
    status: "diagnosed_live_resource_stall_exact_allocator_origin_unconfirmed"
    objective: >-
      Inspect Kevin's live Ciel Runtime and Codex session after reports that
      Codex pauses for a long time following the recent nightly upgrade.

  remote_scope:
    host: "kevin-codex@100.95.132.58"
    hostname: "aap-pool-hera"
    tmux_session: "aap-agent-89"
    workspace: "/home/kevin-codex"
    observed_utc: "2026-08-31T01:16:18Z through 2026-08-31T01:23Z"
    ciel_version: "0.2.36-nightly.20260828-200425.98ea93f"
    codex_version: "0.151.0"
    codex_thread: "01a03716-9bb1-77e2-8771-4329c3b0338f"
    ciel_cli_pid: 2166378
    router_pid: 2168063
    codex_pid: 2168133

  confirmed_observations:
    user_provided_post_termination_measurement:
      screenshot: >-
        C:\Users\djlov\AppData\Local\Temp\codex-clipboard-aO0tdc.png
      measurements:
        - >-
          The report attributes 10.36 GiB to python3 ciel_runtime.py cli after
          approximately one hour, versus 0.49 GiB to Codex itself.
        - >-
          It reports total Kevin-codex memory falling from 17.78 GiB to 6.01 GiB
          after terminating that Ciel process, a reduction of 11.88 GiB.
        - >-
          It also reports swap-in falling from approximately 200,000 KiB/s to
          1,596 KiB/s, free RAM rising from 226 MiB to 2,826 MiB, and load falling
          from 15.66 to 6.88.
      evidence_boundary: >-
        This is direct process-attribution evidence that the Ciel CLI allocation
        caused the machine pressure and apparent input stall. A before/after
        termination snapshot alone does not distinguish monotonically unreachable
        objects (a classical leak) from one oversized transcript allocation whose
        freed arenas remain reserved by the Python/glibc allocator.
    process_liveness:
      - "tmux pane aap-agent-89:0.0 remained alive and attached."
      - >-
        Codex PID 2168133 remained alive, retained established local router and
        upstream TLS sockets, and changed its TUI from Working (4m) to later
        tool activity and finally a completed Korean result during observation.
      - >-
        Therefore the observed pause was not a confirmed Codex exit, router
        disconnect, or permanently dead turn.
    active_rollout:
      path: >-
        /home/kevin-codex/.codex/sessions/2026/08/24/
        rollout-2026-08-24T23-03-43-01a03716-9bb1-77e2-8771-4329c3b0338f.jsonl
      size_bytes_samples:
        - 1912566037
        - 1913714178
        - 1913915284
      identity: >-
        The first session_meta record names the same resumed thread ID and cwd
        /home/kevin-codex.
    ciel_parent_memory:
      - >-
        Ciel CLI PID 2166378 was repeatedly observed at approximately 3.3 GiB
        through 6.5 GiB RSS, with earlier /proc status showing 6.3 GiB of swap
        and VmPeak 14,854,516 KiB.
      - >-
        smaps_rollup showed 4,492,012 KiB anonymous private dirty memory in one
        sample. pmap showed two dominant anonymous mappings, approximately
        3,445,780 KiB and 7,475,388 KiB virtual size.
      - >-
        The same process alternated between runnable state and uninterruptible
        D state at folio_wait_bit_common.
      - >-
        Its /proc I/O counters showed rchar fixed at 14,290,753,604 bytes while
        read_bytes rose from 44,797,825,024 to 46,433,374,208 in 20 seconds.
        During a later idle TUI interval, read_bytes rose from 52,806,254,592
        to 54,489,292,800 while rchar changed by only 2,578 bytes. This is
        direct evidence of storage reads without equivalent userspace file
        reads, consistent with faulting anonymous pages back from swap.
      - >-
        PID 2166378 accumulated 1,776,955 major faults by 01:22Z.
    host_pressure:
      - "Memory was 13 GiB used of 14 GiB with only 209-268 MiB free in samples."
      - "Swap was 25 GiB used of 27 GiB in multiple samples."
      - >-
        vmstat samples showed roughly 70-93 MiB/s swap-in and 89-162 MiB/s
        swap-out with CPU idle at zero during the strongest pressure sample.
      - >-
        PSI reported memory some avg10=16.59%, memory full avg10=9.88%, I/O
        some avg10=31.75%, and I/O full avg10=9.10%.
      - >-
        cgroup memory.events remained at cumulative oom_kill=21 during this
        inspection; no new incident-specific OOM kill was observed.
    version_diff:
      - >-
        The installed Ciel commit is 98ea93f. The two commits after Kevin's
        previously observed 23ae8b0 build are 0b3daa2 (POSIX child-exit
        evidence) and 98ea93f (Windows manual TUI draft preservation).
      - >-
        git diff 23ae8b0..98ea93f contains no change to Codex session selection,
        transcript incremental reading, or Linux memory allocation. This does
        not support a confirmed direct causal claim that either recent Ciel
        patch introduced the large heap.

  diagnosis:
    confirmed_current_stall_mechanism: >-
      Kevin's long apparent Codex pauses coincide with severe host memory and
      I/O pressure. The largest process is the Ciel CLI parent, not Codex. It
      repeatedly faults gigabytes of anonymous memory through swap and enters
      folio_wait_bit_common, while the Codex child remains alive and eventually
      completes the turn.
    upgrade_causality: >-
      Not established. The upgrade did restart Ciel and resume the 1.91 GB
      thread, but the inspected Ciel commit diff does not modify the relevant
      Linux transcript/memory paths. Temporal ordering alone is not evidence
      that the two recent patches caused the allocation.
    exact_allocation_origin: >-
      Unconfirmed. The running host has no gdb, py-spy, strace, perf, pstack,
      or readable /proc PID stack, so the live Python allocation stack could
      not be captured without installing or injecting a profiler.

  source_risk_found:
    file: "ciel_runtime_support/channel_transcript_repository.py"
    lines: "64-97"
    confirmed_behavior: >-
      read_turn_updates seeks to its stored offset and then calls unbounded
      stream.read(), followed by bytes slicing, UTF-8 decode, and splitlines in
      active_turn_from_text. If the offset is zero or reset for a large resumed
      transcript, this path can materialize the entire unread suffix.
    evidence_boundary: >-
      This is a verified code-level memory hazard and it matches the shape of
      the giant anonymous mappings, but the live Python allocation stack was
      unavailable. It is therefore a leading candidate, not a proven origin of
      PID 2166378's current heap.
    history: >-
      The unbounded incremental-read implementation was introduced in commit
      a6b8c58 on 2026-08-20, not in the two most recent commits installed by
      Kevin.
    resolution: >-
      Locally replaced on 2026-08-30 with a 4 MiB per-poll incremental reader,
      bounded oversized-record discard state, and lazy physical-line iteration.
      Controlled RSS and full-project tests passed; Kevin deployment remains pending.

  remote_mutations:
    performed: false
    note: >-
      No process was killed or restarted, no package was updated, and no
      remote file or configuration was changed during this diagnosis.

  local_mutations:
    - >-
      Added this required diagnostic journal only. Existing unrelated working
      tree modifications were left untouched.
